task: encode-side-depth-safety
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/encode-side-depth-safety
worktree: ../okay-wt-encode-depth
spec: specs/iterative-recursive-decode.md, remove-codecs-maxdepth (its
  direct follow-up — found while answering an operator question about
  Cont fusion, not from a bug report)

why: `remove-codecs-maxdepth` made every DECODE door stack-safe at any
  input depth, but never touched the WRITE side. `Json.print` and
  `Cbor.put`/`write` are still plain native recursion over the value
  being written — the exact same shape the decode arc fixed, just on
  the other end of the pipe. Before this lane, that recursion was
  reachable only by a value someone built directly in memory (rare);
  now that decode itself can hand back an arbitrarily deep value from
  untrusted network input, decode-then-reencode (a proxy, a relay, a
  log) crashes on the write half of a round trip decode already
  proved safe. Two more native-recursion sites found by the same grep
  that found this: `Json.mergePatch` (recurses on PATCH depth, RFC
  7396) and `okay-demo/StateMcp.damaged` (a byte-for-byte duplicate of
  the `okay-mcp/Rpc.damaged` bug remove-codecs-maxdepth already fixed
  once).

what: `Json.print`, `Cbor.put`/`Cbor.write`, `Json.mergePatch` get the
  same `Codecs.NativeThreshold`-then-`Cont.defer` split the decode
  doors already carry (`Json.into`/`intoC` in the same file is the
  exact template: side-effect into a mutable builder/Out, `Unit`-
  returning trampoline). `okay-demo/StateMcp.damaged` gets the same
  explicit work-list rewrite `okay-mcp/Rpc.damaged` already got.

predict: mechanical, following an established in-file template
  (`into`/`intoC`) for three of the four sites; the fourth
  (`StateMcp.damaged`) is a straight copy of an already-landed fix.
  Needs new tests proving each function safe on a genuinely deep
  IN-MEMORY value (built directly, not decoded, so the test does not
  depend on decode's own depth safety) — a large recursive schema
  value is easy to build without going through Cbor/Json's actual
  parser.
